# dorfl — the `advance` loop in CI (the advance-loop capability, SPEC advance-loop, US #27/28)
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADOPTION: copy THIS file to `.github/workflows/advance-loop.yml` in your target
# repo, set the secrets it references, and add the files it calls, which
# `dorfl install-ci` generates (`dorfl install-ci --fake` writes them to `.fake/`
# for a look first): `.github/workflows/dorfl-item-dispatch.yml`,
# `.github/workflows/dorfl-item.yml`, and the setup actions
# `.github/actions/dorfl-setup` (agent role) and
# `.github/actions/dorfl-setup-writer` (writer role). It is a `.template` here ON
# PURPOSE so it NEVER self-triggers inside the dorfl repo itself (a live
# `.github/workflows/*.yml` here would loop the tool on its own work — see
# docs/ci/README.md "Why a `.template`").
#
# THIS IS ONE CAPABILITY, not the whole CI story. The unified per-capability
# `install-ci` CLI (auth/secrets wizard + GitHub adapter; auto-build/auto-slice,
# this advance loop, issue intake, the close-job, the gc sweep) is owned by the
# `runner-in-ci` SPEC (work/specs/tasked/runner-in-ci.md); it EMITS this template
# as its advance-loop capability. Edit the workflow shape HERE to change what that
# CLI emits. See docs/ci/README.md "Relationship to the `install-ci` CLI".
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHAT IT DOES. It only INVOKES the existing `advance` driver (the substrate-agnostic
# tick wrapped by the one-shot driver), through the per-item workflow — it adds NO
# new execution model. Triggers: a cron schedule AND a push to main that ANSWERS a
# question (a commit touching `work/questions/*`, so a freshly-answered sidecar is
# applied promptly), plus a manual `workflow_dispatch`.
#
# THE SPLIT (ADR ci-agent-job-holds-no-write-token). THIS workflow runs no agent:
# `enumerate` lists the eligible items, and `dispatch` starts ONE
# `dorfl-item-dispatch.yml` run per item, which calls `dorfl-item.yml`:
#   * a LOCK job (write token, no agent, no repository code) takes the item's
#     locks at the arbiter's current main;
#   * an AGENT job (read-only token, `persist-credentials: false`) runs the agents
#     and uploads a handoff;
#   * an APPLY job (write token, no agent, no repository code) validates the
#     handoff as hostile and does every write.
# One workflow RUN per item (not a matrix): all jobs of one run share one artifact
# namespace, so a matrix would let one item's agent plant another item's handoff.
# The parallelism cap is `maxParallel` concurrency SLOTS on the item runs
# (`dorfl-slot-<index mod maxParallel>`, `queue: max`): at most that many item runs
# execute at once and the rest wait first-in-first-out, costing no runner minutes.
#
# ONE WORD, ONE MEANING — `integrationMode` (US #27). The dispatch input is the
# INTEGRATION MODE (propose | merge, the same vocabulary as `dorfl.json`'s
# `integration` and `advance --propose`/`--merge`). The `dispatch` job forwards it
# to every item run, which passes it to `advance`, so the mode the items run in can
# NEVER desync from the tick:
#
#   * `integrationMode: propose` → each item opens its OWN PR (an item run can
#                       NEVER merge to main). The items are ENUMERATED via the
#                       pool scan (`dorfl scan --json`), so CI fans out over
#                       exactly the eligible pool.
#
#   * `integrationMode: merge` → each item lands on main. Build, gate and review
#                       run CONCURRENTLY across item runs; the LAND TAIL
#                       (rebase-before-integrate + CAS push to `main`, ADR §10) is
#                       what serialises, by the engine's safety story, NOT by this
#                       workflow's shape.
#
#                       THE FLOOR (git-alone, host-agnostic): the cross-run
#                       serialiser is the engine's `mergeRetries` CAS-retry loop
#                       (`integration-core.ts` + `merge-retries-gate-precedence`),
#                       run by the item's APPLY job. A non-fast-forward push
#                       triggers a re-rebase and a retry up to the resolved cap —
#                       never a `--force`, never a both-land-broken. A lost CAS does
#                       NOT re-run the gate: the gate ran in the agent job minutes
#                       earlier, so in merge mode most lands are re-rebased after
#                       it. The apply phase reports every land whose tree differs
#                       from the gated tree ("landed without re-gate after N lost
#                       races") in its output and in the landed commit's trailer.
#                       Raise `mergeRetries` for a wider slot count.
#
#                       NO `concurrency:` SERIALISER ON MAIN. The slot groups cap
#                       the parallelism only; a host-specific group serialising the
#                       land would be load-bearing for safety, and the floor is
#                       supposed to be safe on a bare arbiter with no host.
#
# Pick the mode with the `integrationMode` dispatch input (default below). It rides
# `advance` as `--propose`/`--merge`, which sits at the TOP of the precedence chain
# (flag > per-repo `dorfl.json` `integration` > global > default), so the
# WORKFLOW mode always wins over a repo's config default — they cannot disagree.

name: advance-loop

on:
  schedule:
    # Cron tick: drain whatever the human has answered since the last run.
    # Adjust the cadence to taste (here: hourly).
    - cron: '0 * * * *'
  push:
    # On-answer-committed: a push to the default branch that touches an answered
    # question sidecar (`work/questions/*`) re-runs the loop so the answer is
    # applied promptly. Pinned to main: a work-branch push touching a sidecar must
    # never run the loop on that branch.
    branches:
      - main
    paths:
      - 'work/questions/**'
  workflow_dispatch:
    inputs:
      integrationMode:
        description: "Integration mode, forwarded to every item run as `advance --propose`/`--merge`: propose ⇒ one PR per item; merge ⇒ each item lands on main (the LAND tail is serialised by the engine's `mergeRetries` CAS-retry loop, not by the workflow shape)."
        required: false
        default: 'propose'
        type: choice
        options:
          - propose
          - merge
      sweepMergedBranches:
        description: 'Reap merged remote work/* branches on the scheduled tick (gc --remote-branches): delete only branches PROVABLY MERGED into <arbiter>/main, so out-of-band human/UI PR merges stop leaving their work/<slug> branches lingering on the arbiter. ON by default; set false to opt out. An in-flight (un-merged) branch is NEVER touched.'
        required: false
        default: true
        type: boolean
      # ── GATE-FAMILY one-shot overrides (dispatch only) ──────────────────────
      # Override an engine gate for THIS manual run only (rides the env layer of
      # flag > env > per-repo > global > default). `type: choice` with a BLANK first
      # option (not `type: boolean`, which has no "unset"): blank ⇒ emit NOTHING
      # (config wins); a non-blank choice ⇒ export the matching DORFL_* in
      # `enumerate` and forward it to every item run.
      autoBuild:
        description: 'One-shot override of the autoBuild gate (DORFL_AUTO_BUILD) for THIS dispatch run only. Blank ⇒ no override (config wins).'
        required: false
        default: ''
        type: choice
        options:
          - ''
          - 'true'
          - 'false'
      autoTask:
        description: 'One-shot override of the autoTask gate (DORFL_AUTO_TASK) for THIS dispatch run only. Blank ⇒ no override (config wins).'
        required: false
        default: ''
        type: choice
        options:
          - ''
          - 'true'
          - 'false'
      observationTriage:
        description: 'One-shot override of the observationTriage gate (DORFL_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE) for THIS dispatch run only. Blank ⇒ no override (config wins).'
        required: false
        default: ''
        type: choice
        options:
          - ''
          - 'off'
          - 'ask'
          - 'auto'
      surfaceBlockers:
        description: 'One-shot override of the surfaceBlockers gate (DORFL_SURFACE_BLOCKERS) for THIS dispatch run only. Blank ⇒ no override (config wins).'
        required: false
        default: ''
        type: choice
        options:
          - ''
          - 'true'
          - 'false'

# Serialise overlapping ticks of the same ref; the claim CAS is the real
# cross-run serialiser, this just avoids redundant concurrent ticks.
concurrency:
  group: advance-loop-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: false

# Nothing at workflow level: each job grants its own scopes.
permissions: {}

jobs:
  # ── ENUMERATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # List the eligible items. `scan --json` reads BOTH the registry/hub-mirror
  # pool (`repos[].items[]` + `repos[].specs[]`) AND the in-place working checkout
  # (`cwd.repo.items[]` + `cwd.repo.specs[]`); CI runs IN-PLACE so the eligible
  # items live in the latter (a fresh runner has no registered mirror). `jq`
  # unions + dedups both eligible-TASK pools AND both taskable-SPEC pools into a
  # list of explicit `task:<slug>` / `spec:<slug>` ids. Read-only: it runs no agent.
  enumerate:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: read
    outputs:
      items: ${{ steps.scan.outputs.items }}
      any: ${{ steps.scan.outputs.any }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
      - uses: ./.github/actions/dorfl-setup-writer
      - name: apply dispatch gate overrides (one-shot, this run only)
        # MUST run BEFORE `scan`: scan's pools are gated by the SAME engine gate
        # family, so an override that does not reach this job empties the item
        # list and is silently inert. `if:` + the inner `[ -n ... ]` guard keep
        # schedule/push (and a blank dispatch field) exporting NOTHING — an empty
        # DORFL_* would make the engine's env coercion throw. The inputs reach the
        # shell through `env:` as DATA, never as `${{ }}` text spliced into the
        # script, and the `case` guards stop a value from appending its own lines
        # to $GITHUB_ENV.
        if: ${{ github.event_name == 'workflow_dispatch' }}
        env:
          DISPATCH_AUTO_BUILD: ${{ github.event.inputs.autoBuild }}
          DISPATCH_AUTO_TASK: ${{ github.event.inputs.autoTask }}
          DISPATCH_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE: ${{ github.event.inputs.observationTriage }}
          DISPATCH_SURFACE_BLOCKERS: ${{ github.event.inputs.surfaceBlockers }}
        run: |
          case "${DISPATCH_AUTO_BUILD}${DISPATCH_AUTO_TASK}${DISPATCH_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE}${DISPATCH_SURFACE_BLOCKERS}" in
            *$'\n'*|*$'\r'*) echo "::error::a dispatch gate override contains a line break"; exit 1 ;;
          esac
          [ -n "${DISPATCH_AUTO_BUILD}" ] && echo "DORFL_AUTO_BUILD=${DISPATCH_AUTO_BUILD}" >> "$GITHUB_ENV"
          [ -n "${DISPATCH_AUTO_TASK}" ] && echo "DORFL_AUTO_TASK=${DISPATCH_AUTO_TASK}" >> "$GITHUB_ENV"
          [ -n "${DISPATCH_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE}" ] && echo "DORFL_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE=${DISPATCH_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE}" >> "$GITHUB_ENV"
          [ -n "${DISPATCH_SURFACE_BLOCKERS}" ] && echo "DORFL_SURFACE_BLOCKERS=${DISPATCH_SURFACE_BLOCKERS}" >> "$GITHUB_ENV"
          true
      - id: scan
        # The eligible-pool scan: enumerate eligible items as namespaced ids
        # (`task:<slug>` builds, `spec:<slug>` auto-slices, capability B).
        run: |
          items="$(dorfl scan --json --here \
            | jq -c '[(.repos[].items[]?, .cwd.repo.items[]?) | select(.eligibility.eligible == true) | "task:" + .slug] + [(.repos[].specs[]?, .cwd.repo.specs[]?) | select(.eligibility.eligible == true) | "spec:" + .slug] | unique')"
          echo "items=${items}" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          if [ "$(echo "${items}" | jq 'length')" -gt 0 ]; then
            echo "any=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          else
            echo "any=false" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          fi

  # ── DISPATCH: one dorfl-item-dispatch.yml run per item ──────────────────────
  # `actions: write` and NOTHING else, NO checkout and NO setup: no repository
  # code runs next to `actions: write`, only the runner's preinstalled `gh` and
  # `jq`. Each item run joins the slot dorfl-slot-<index mod MAX_PARALLEL>
  # (`queue: max`); an item whose `dorfl-item <item>` run has not completed yet is
  # SKIPPED (a duplicate that slips through a race is harmless: its lock job
  # re-classifies at the fresh arbiter tip). CI / automation MUST use explicit
  # prefixes (`task:foo` / `spec:foo`), never bare (ADR
  # command-surface-and-journeys §"CI uses prefixes").
  dispatch:
    needs: enumerate
    if: ${{ needs.enumerate.outputs.any == 'true' }}
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      actions: write
    steps:
      - name: dispatch one item run per item
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
          REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
          DEFAULT_BRANCH: ${{ github.event.repository.default_branch || 'main' }}
          ITEMS: ${{ needs.enumerate.outputs.items }}
          # Cap concurrent item runs: each spawns a FULL agent session (build +
          # Gate-2 review), so an unbounded fan-out exhausts the model provider's
          # API rate limit AND thrashes the CAS. The `install-ci` CLI parameterises
          # this via `--max-parallel`.
          MAX_PARALLEL: '4'
          INTEGRATION_MODE: ${{ github.event.inputs.integrationMode || 'propose' }}
          DISPATCH_AUTO_BUILD: ${{ github.event.inputs.autoBuild }}
          DISPATCH_AUTO_TASK: ${{ github.event.inputs.autoTask }}
          DISPATCH_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE: ${{ github.event.inputs.observationTriage }}
          DISPATCH_SURFACE_BLOCKERS: ${{ github.event.inputs.surfaceBlockers }}
        run: |
          set -euo pipefail
          active="$(gh run list -R "${REPO}" --workflow dorfl-item-dispatch.yml --limit 1000 --json displayTitle,status --jq '.[] | select(.status != "completed") | .displayTitle')"
          mapfile -t items < <(printf '%s' "${ITEMS}" | jq -r '.[]')
          i=0
          for item in "${items[@]}"; do
            slot=$(( i % MAX_PARALLEL ))
            i=$(( i + 1 ))
            if printf '%s\n' "${active}" | grep -Fxq -- "dorfl-item ${item}"; then
              echo "skipped    ${item}  slot ${slot}  (its 'dorfl-item ${item}' run is not completed)"
              continue
            fi
            args=(-f "item=${item}" -f "slot=${slot}" -f "integrationMode=${INTEGRATION_MODE}")
            if [ -n "${DISPATCH_AUTO_BUILD}" ]; then args+=(-f "autoBuild=${DISPATCH_AUTO_BUILD}"); fi
            if [ -n "${DISPATCH_AUTO_TASK}" ]; then args+=(-f "autoTask=${DISPATCH_AUTO_TASK}"); fi
            if [ -n "${DISPATCH_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE}" ]; then args+=(-f "observationTriage=${DISPATCH_OBSERVATION_TRIAGE}"); fi
            if [ -n "${DISPATCH_SURFACE_BLOCKERS}" ]; then args+=(-f "surfaceBlockers=${DISPATCH_SURFACE_BLOCKERS}"); fi
            gh workflow run dorfl-item-dispatch.yml -R "${REPO}" --ref "${DEFAULT_BRANCH}" "${args[@]}"
            echo "dispatched ${item}  slot ${slot}  (run 'dorfl-item ${item}')"
          done

  # ── REAP merged remote work/* branches (the hygiene sweep) ───────────────────
  # The provider-agnostic counterpart of the worktree reaper: delete remote
  # `work/<slug>` branches that are PROVABLY MERGED into `origin/main`
  # (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`), so an out-of-band human/UI PR merge stops
  # leaving its branch lingering on the arbiter. Independent of the integration
  # mode, so it runs on EVERY tick regardless of `integrationMode` — gated ONLY by
  # the `sweepMergedBranches` opt-out (ON by default). It NEVER touches an
  # un-merged/in-flight branch (the predicate excludes it) and NEVER `--force`s.
  # Plain git, so it works against any provider (incl. a `--bare` arbiter). It
  # runs no agent: the writer-role setup (Node and dorfl only).
  #
  # OPTIONAL belt-and-suspenders for a GitHub arbiter: enable the repo-level
  # "Automatically delete head branches" setting (`delete_branch_on_merge`) too
  # (Settings → General, or `gh api -X PATCH repos/{owner}/{repo} -f
  # delete_branch_on_merge=true`). That is an ADDITIVE GitHub-only convenience for
  # PRs merged through GitHub's own UI — NOT a replacement for this sweep, which is
  # the general home and the only thing that reaps branches on a `--bare` /
  # non-GitHub arbiter or after a merge done outside GitHub.
  reap-merged-branches:
    if: ${{ (github.event.inputs.sweepMergedBranches || 'true') == 'true' }}
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: write
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1
        with:
          fetch-depth: 0
      - uses: ./.github/actions/dorfl-setup-writer
      - name: reap merged remote work/* branches + orphan sidecars (gc --remote-branches)
        # Deletes ONLY branches provably merged into origin/main; reports
        # deleted-vs-retained-with-reason. Safe to run every tick (idempotent).
        # This invocation ALSO reaps ORPHAN question sidecars: a
        # sidecar under the questions queue (<type>-<slug>.md) whose source item
        # was deleted out-of-band (git rm; the sidecar's working-tree source no
        # longer exists). The orphan sweep rides THIS scheduled invocation
        # precisely so it is never "in the code but never invoked in CI".
        run: dorfl gc --remote-branches --arbiter origin
